WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.320
What exactly did it take for Heath Ledger to pull&nbsp;
off his Oscar-winning Joker performance? Does the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:11.520
diary he kept at the time hold any clues about&nbsp;
his tragic death? Keep watching for the answers.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.360
Over a decade after it first appeared on the&nbsp;
big screen, Heath Ledger's rendition of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:23.520
Joker in 2008's "The Dark Knight" stands&nbsp;
above all other supervillain portrayals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:30.080
He came across as cold, thoughtful, and scarily&nbsp;
real, bringing the Clown Prince of Crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:36.560
to life in a new and impactful way. Pulling off&nbsp;
such a chilling performance didn't come easy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.880
and was the result of impressive dedication&nbsp;
on the part of the man behind the makeup.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:45.360
Heath Ledger took his acting work incredibly&nbsp;
seriously and didn't shy away from an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.680
opportunity to push himself as an artist.&nbsp;
He'd envelop himself in his characters to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:56.640
ensure he got every mannerism, line, and facial&nbsp;
expression just right, no matter the nature of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:02.240
the project. Director Christopher Nolan's vision&nbsp;
of Joker was no exception, and as it turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:07.120
Ledger took his methodology to the next&nbsp;
level for the part with a disturbing diary.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.840
While much of the diary's content – playing&nbsp;
cards, comic panels, and various quotes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.400
from "The Dark Knight" – centers on the&nbsp;
Joker, it also contains many extra images&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:21.920
and writings. These give fans a glimpse&nbsp;
into Ledger's mind as he delved deeper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.680
down the rabbit hole. The small book has become&nbsp;
almost as legendary as the character itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:33.600
leading fans and critics to praise Ledger for his&nbsp;
unsettling commitment to his craft. Because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:39.200
the foreboding nature of its content, however,&nbsp;
it comes with its fair share of urban legends.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:45.680
To truly become the Joker, Heath Ledger chose&nbsp;
to isolate himself from the rest of the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:50.960
for months at a time, locking himself in his&nbsp;
apartment or hotel room to iron out the finer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:56.320
details of the character. During his exile,&nbsp;
he'd work on his diary free of distraction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:01.680
He documented his thought process, important&nbsp;
quirks to the character, and other notes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:07.200
describe how he came up with so many now-iconic&nbsp;
parts of the Joker's characterization.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.640
In a German documentary about the late actor,&nbsp;
titled Too Young to Die: Heath Ledger, his father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.920
Kim Ledger, was interviewed on the unorthodox&nbsp;
nature of his son's preparation. While the elder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:23.840
Ledger admitted that the isolation and prolonged&nbsp;
study was nothing new for his son, he described&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:31.040
the Joker journal as being on a whole new level.&nbsp;
Even as bizarre as its content seemed on its own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:37.280
the two strategies Ledger utilized in tandem were&nbsp;
undeniably crucial to perfecting his performance.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.880
The diary is full of clippings from comic&nbsp;
books, which Ledger read in isolation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:47.840
Pages of the diary are filled with handwritten&nbsp;
versions of the Joker's dialogue from his scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:52.160
including the infamous moment when the&nbsp;
Joker detonates a bomb in a Gotham City&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.280
hospital. In the documentary, Kim&nbsp;
mentions that when they were kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.680
Ledger's older sister Kate would dress him&nbsp;
in a nurse's uniform, like he wears in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:05.200
movie. When Ledger described his version of&nbsp;
the character to Empire in 2007, he said,

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:10.560
"I ended up landing more in the realm of a&nbsp;
psychopath. Someone with very little to no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:18.320
conscience towards his acts. He's just an absolute&nbsp;
sociopath, a cold-blooded, mass-murdering clown.”

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:22.960
“Ah-ah-ah. Let’s not blow this out of proportion.”

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:27.440
Ledger also told Empire about Christopher&nbsp;
Nolan's input on the character. Ledger said,

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:32.400
"Chris has given me free rein. Which is&nbsp;
fun, because there are no real boundaries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:38.320
to what The Joker would say or do. Nothing&nbsp;
intimidates him, and everything is a big joke."

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.920
On set, Ledger often improvised&nbsp;
moments during scenes, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.160
Ledger rarely told Christopher Nolan&nbsp;
exactly what he was planning to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:51.040
The unpredictability made directing his scenes&nbsp;
much more intense than usual. One of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:55.360
memorable moments in the film that Ledger&nbsp;
improvised takes place during the scene when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:00.320
Jim Gordon, played by Gary Oldman, gets promoted&nbsp;
to police commissioner shortly after capturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:06.000
the Joker. The Joker, in a nearby prison cell,&nbsp;
sarcastically claps along with everyone else.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:13.120
Among the many seemingly random images and&nbsp;
frantic scribblings inside Ledger's diary were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:19.840
a handful of references to Stanley Kubrick's 1971&nbsp;
classic "A Clockwork Orange." More specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:24.880
there were a few images of fictional psychopath&nbsp;
Alex DeLarge. The main character of the piece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:30.320
he's best described as a sadistic teenager&nbsp;
who commits heinous crimes and terrorizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:37.360
anyone and everyone he encounters simply for the&nbsp;
fun of it. Is that description ringing any bells?

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.440
Another one of the most important things to get&nbsp;
right when developing a new Joker adaptation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.840
is the character's laugh. Every actor that has&nbsp;
stepped into that role put a different spin on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:51.840
it that fit that specific incarnation perfectly.&nbsp;
Ledger knew he couldn't simply copy Mark Hamill or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.160
Jack Nicholson, as that would cheapen the&nbsp;
character, so he had to do some digging and create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:02.080
his own laugh from scratch. As his diary detailed,&nbsp;
he found his muse in the form of a hyena.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:06.240
The hyena is synonymous with its&nbsp;
"laugh," a call the species uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:10.640
to express any number of emotions. The&nbsp;
sound is generally high-pitched and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:15.120
comes out as a threatening cackle that's&nbsp;
perfect for the Clown Prince of Crime.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:19.360
[Joker laughing]

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:23.840
In Ledger's journal, there was at least&nbsp;
one picture of a laughing hyena included,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:28.480
most likely citing his interest in using their&nbsp;
"laugh" as a starting point for his own. Looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:33.840
back on his performance, the similarities between&nbsp;
the two vocal expressions are rather striking.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:39.520
On January 22, 2008, Heath Ledger&nbsp;
tragically passed away at the young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:44.000
age of 28 due to cardiac arrest. According&nbsp;
to the medical examiner, his passing came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:48.160
as a result of an overdose of prescription&nbsp;
medication that brought on a heart attack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:52.480
It was no secret that Ledger went to&nbsp;
serious extremes to slip into character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:58.560
but did it contribute to his untimely death? After&nbsp;
all, the last entry did say, quote, "BYE BYE."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:03.600
So, was there any validity to the claims&nbsp;
that Joker pushed Ledger over the edge?

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:08.400
Simply put, despite his journal's cryptic nature&nbsp;
and his devotion to the Joker role, Ledger's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:13.760
death wasn't a direct cause of his preparation.&nbsp;
His drug abuse issues weren't a new phenomenon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:18.800
and his debut in the DC universe wasn't driving&nbsp;
him into any kind of depression. His sister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:22.240
Kate Ledger, set the record straight on&nbsp;
her brother's attitude towards the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:25.520
telling reporters at the&nbsp;
2017 Tribeca Film Festival,

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:27.600
"He wasn't depressed about the Joker."

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:32.160
His Joker diary was very concerning out of&nbsp;
context, but it turned out to be nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:37.200
more than a tool by which Ledger was able&nbsp;
to pull off the performance of a lifetime.

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:41.840
If you or someone you know is struggling with&nbsp;
addiction, please call the Substance Abuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:54.960
and Mental Health Administration's 24/7 National&nbsp;
Helpline at 1-800-662-HELP. That's 1-800-662-4357.

